Nov 06, 2026
 
 
 😧 TGIF? Yesterday, U.S. tech stocks lost trillions (trillions!) of dollars in market value as the AI trade appeared to unravel. 

 This morning, U.S. futures are up a bit with the S&amp;P 500 +0.25% and Nasdaq 100 futures +1.6%. That's after Asian markets fell overnight with the AI-heavy Kospi in South Korea down 6% and Japan's Nikkei in bear market territory, or down about 20% since its June high. Oh, and in about an hour, we get the October employment report. 🕺🏻 Is the music about to stop? Is this a moment akin to the popping of the internet bubble or the floor dropping out of the housing market? We will see, but clearly now all companies tied to the AI boom are being warily scrutinized. 

 As Matt explains below, a repricing of something as enormous as the investment in AI is really, really, really hard to do without things falling apart. Courage, dear readers. In 992 words, a 3.5-minute read. 


 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 
1 big thing: A boom too big to rein in
 
 By Matt Phillips 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet; Chart: Matt Phillips/Axios Slowing an investment boom on the scale of the AI buildout is a tricky business — as yesterday's rout in the stock market demonstrated.

 Why it matters: Americans may have voted in the midterms to tap the brakes on the AI race, but curbing the investment boom — or some might say, gently deflating the AI bubble — is easier said than done. 

 The big picture: The historical record shows that once major investment booms are in motion, it can be difficult to temper them without sparking a stampede of investors out of assets.

 That's why market collapses have featured prominently in past investment booms like those in canals, railroads and the internet. "We can't control it," John D. Turner, a finance professor at Queen's University Belfast who wrote "Boom and Bust: A Global History of Financial Bubbles," tells Axios. "People just get spooked, and as soon as they do, then, you know, prices head down. And that's what happens in historical bubbles, and it can happen quite quickly." Catch up quick: The overwhelming rejection of AI in this week's elections — from wins for AI-cautious Democrats in governors' races in hotbeds of development to overwhelming support for ballot measures restricting data center construction — has pushed investors to reevaluate their previous bets on companies profiting from the data center boom. 

 Stunning stat: Over 50% of the planned data center capacity is in just three states, Ohio, Georgia and Texas — all states where Democrats just defeated Republicans in gubernatorial races, according to Fitch Ratings.

 "If you were thinking that there might be some issues when it came to AI investment in the first place— and the AI build-out — well, now you have three key states, all with new governors-elect who are not friendly to the cause," says Steve Sosnick, chief strategist at Interactive Brokers. Data: U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis; Chart: Matt Phillips/Axios 
 
 
