2. The reckoning in AI stocks
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet; Chart: Matt Phillips/Axios Tech stocks plunged as the week has developed into a major test for the market. 

 The Nasdaq Composite Index fell 5.5% yesterday — its worst daily showing since the quasi-panic of President Trump's "liberation day" tariffs in April 2025 — and is down roughly 10% from the high it hit a month ago. On Wall Street, a drop of that magnitude is known as a "correction," a term of art indicating a selloff more serious than a garden-variety decline. The Nasdaq 100, a measure of the largest tech stocks, is now down roughly 15% since its Oct. 6 peak. Its 6.4% drop yesterday was its steepest since the March 2020 pandemic panic. The SOX semiconductor index also endured its worst day since the pandemic, plummeting 12.3% yesterday. The broader S&amp;P 500 index slumped 4.4%. Reality check: The 4-year-old bull market for the blue chips still holds: The S&amp;P 500 is off 6% from its high and is up 7.4% on the year. 

 Zoom in: Many of the proposed constraints on AI are limited in nature and do not apply to projects already underway. Even so, the market reaction has been ferocious. 

 Stocks tied to the AI build-out were hit particularly hard yesterday, with makers of everything from fiber-optic connections (Coherent down 13%, Ciena down 15%), to HVAC systems (Comfort Systems, -11.6%), servers (Dell dove 15%) and semiconductors (Advanced Micro Devices, -17.3%) knocked down, some after enduring deep losses Wednesday. Engineering, electrical and industrial equipment stocks closely tied to the boom also collapsed. Quanta Services and Vertiv Holdings plunged 12% and 14%, respectively. The so-called hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta and Oracle — were all down too, though their losses were less lurid, as they could likely benefit from any slowdown in AI spending as a result of increased regulatory scrutiny. Between the lines: To some, the sudden tumble in the markets — futures were actually up a bit in pre-market trading yesterday — suggested that a large institutional investor may have suddenly moved to sell a sizable chunk of a portfolio that had turned south after Wednesday's post-election slump in AI shares. 

 Hedge funds had already been selling, as they unwound positions last month at their highest rate in over a decade, per a Morgan Stanley note cited by the Wall Street Journal . Elsewhere, retail investors, who in past market downturns have swarmed in to buy the dip, instead dumped shares, acting as heavy sellers of tech stocks yesterday, said Jai Malhi, senior equity strategist at Vanda Research, which tracks trading by individual investors. What we're watching: For any indications that the volatility hitting the AI trade could prompt Anthropic and rival OpenAI to reconsider plans to offer shares to the public. 

 And will the stock market jitters spread to the more sober-minded markets for corporate debt, where a large chunk of data center construction is expected to be financed? The bottom line: To be continued. 


 
 
